1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,693
Module 17 starts the developer track.

2
00:00:01,693 --> 00:00:07,000
We open the codebase for real, starting with the file almost everything else touches: the app's central state store.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:07,528
src/lib/store.

4
00:00:07,528 --> 00:00:12,623
ts is a single Zustand store: one flat object of state — chats, settings, agents, the current view — plus every action that mutates it.

5
00:00:12,623 --> 00:00:17,000
Components never mutate state directly; they call an action, and the store decides what changes and what re-renders.

6
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:27,405
Take the chat actions as a case study, because you've already seen every one of them in this series: sendMessage and regenerate from Module 2, stop from the same place, branchAt and editUserMessage and rewindTo and deleteItem from Module 11's editing episode, compactChat from Module 15.

7
00:00:27,405 --> 00:00:29,000
Same actions, now with the code behind them.

8
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:29,920
Two are worth a close look.

9
00:00:29,920 --> 00:00:33,359
rewindTo deletes a message and everything after it, but snapshots the chat first, so it's reversible.

10
00:00:33,359 --> 00:00:40,000
deleteItem removes one item — a message, its reasoning, or a single tool call — to trim context, and repairs tool-call pairing at send time so a dangling tool result never gets sent to the model.

11
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:40,964
Now the consumer side.

12
00:00:40,964 --> 00:00:41,446
ChatWindow.

13
00:00:41,446 --> 00:00:46,924
tsx doesn't own any chat logic — it destructures the actions it needs straight off useStore, and every button just calls one.

14
00:00:46,924 --> 00:00:51,000
The component's job is rendering and wiring clicks; the store's job is deciding what happens.

15
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,444
The gotcha: two ways to patch a chat, and they're not interchangeable.

16
00:00:53,444 --> 00:00:55,400
updateChat patches whatever chat is currently on screen.

17
00:00:55,400 --> 00:01:02,000
Streaming must use updateChatById with an explicit id instead, because the user can switch chats mid-reply — patching “the current chat” would land the next token in the wrong conversation.

18
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:04,833
With the frontend's state model down, we cross the process boundary.

19
00:01:04,833 --> 00:01:08,000
Next, a command-by-command tour of the Rust backend the frontend calls into.

